connection had 871304 bytes or more accepted within 1 ms on this darwin/arm64 (net.inet.tcp.sendspace and recvspace
131072, autotuned to 4 MiB), and 2244608 or more on janus.local's Linux 5.14 (tcp_rmem 4096 131072 6291456, tcp_wmem
4096 16384 4194304), measured with a stock-Go probe through janus's pueue build group. So 96 KiB fits each OS's
default with room to spare; Windows is not measured.

The mechanism, read from a goroutine dump taken as the guest printed "stopped" in each short run (5 of 5), with
temporary prints in sockSend.run that were reverted: the response's writes reached the host as 613, 3502, 32768, 32768
and 28754 bytes. The first four were written to the connection, which is 69651 bytes. The last was taken into the
64 KiB send sink (sendBufCap), so the guest's write completed and its handler, Shutdown and exit went on. The writer
goroutine was parked in asyncHandles.acquireRun, called by sockSend.deliver from sockSend.run, delivering its fourth
Write's completion. It got no run slot before Close's producerStop, and then found producersStopped with 28754 bytes
still in its buffer, which Close discards. wasmtime's write completes only once its host has taken the bytes, so the
guest cannot exit ahead of them there.

